from mk import mk
mk("E8b_N1RBCxE","Sound Design Tips From an Orchestral Composer",
 "hybrid-score-five-stage-transform-chain-pitch-formant-heavy-saturation-key-tuned-peak-lp-pushback",
 "Hybrid-score 'sonic signature' chain for any source (orchestral rips, vocal phrases, synth presets, percussion loops, creature SFX): pitch/formant shift → heavy saturation → low-pass with a key-tuned resonant peak → EQ/compression → delay → reverb pushback, plus panning and LFO movement so the result blends behind an orchestra",
 "Transform ordinary material into a unique hybrid-score sound with five tools: pitch shifting (example: +2 semitones with formant −12, or a −12 transpose of a vocal phrase, optionally a robot-type mode mixed in), heavy distortion/saturation (an aggressive mode cranked), filtering (low-pass to clear room for low instruments and cut analog top end, with a resonant peak tuned to the cue's key or around 500 Hz on percussion, before the reverb), then EQ/compression, delay and reverb that push the sound back into the orchestra's space instead of 'in your face'. Add movement (an LFO on filter frequency, an auto-panner with a mix control, subharmonic reinforcement on hits). Works on brass rips, woodwind rips, ethnic vocal phrases, preset 'hunt' results, retro drum loops and animal recordings. More specific than generic FX-chain skills: the orchestral-blend goal, the key-tuned peak and the pre-reverb low-pass.",
 ["Any pitch/formant shifter, saturator/distortion, resonant filter, EQ, compressor, delay and reverb (plugin names in the source are examples only)","Optional: LFO/auto-pan with a mix knob, subharmonic enhancer, resonator/filterbank","A source sound: orchestral sample, vocal phrase, synth preset, percussion loop or field recording"],
 [("source","audio","Sound to transform",True),("cue_key","note","Key of the cue, used to tune the filter's resonant peak",False),("role","enum","pad/atmos, percussion, signature hit, creature/texture",False)],
 [("signature_sound","audio","Transformed hybrid sound that sits behind the orchestra"),("chain","list","Ordered stages and the settings used")],
 ["Order is flexible; the presenter changes it per instrument. Spoken values only: pitch +2 / formant −12, transpose −12, peak around 500 Hz on one percussion loop, key-root peak (e.g. C in C minor).","Low-pass before the reverb to push synths back; avoid bright synth tops 'hitting you in the face' against an orchestra.","Using presets as a starting point is fine, but the transformation chain is the skill. Do not copy preset names into deliverables.","Commit to a sound you like and write around it; sometimes the right move is to degrade a 'better' sound for the cue."],
 [("Pick a source (brass or woodwind rip, vocal phrase, synth preset found by auditioning many, percussion loop or creature SFX). Audition it dry.","source","Reference of the raw sound"),
  ("Pitch stage: shift pitch and/or formant (example +2 st pitch, formant −12; or transpose a vocal −12). Optionally mix in a robot/lo-fi mode with the dry/wet control.","pitch/formant","Character changes beyond simple transposition"),
  ("Distortion stage: an aggressive saturation/'punish' mode cranked, or a clipper preset, until the sound is gnarly.","saturation","Dense, aggressive harmonic character"),
  ("Filter stage: low-pass to cut the analog top end and leave room for low instruments, with a resonant peak at the cue's key (or ~500 Hz on percussion). Several filters in a row or a resonator bank can carve further; add an LFO to filter frequency for movement.","filter","Darker, focused tone with a tuned bump, moving over time"),
  ("Tone/dynamics: EQ (tame low-mid boxiness, lift highs if needed), light compression for loudness; on hits add subharmonic reinforcement.","eq/comp","Controlled level and body"),
  ("Space: add delay, then reverb (plate/hall/large) to push the sound back; add an auto-panner with a mix knob for movement rather than a static patch.","delay/reverb/pan","Sound blends into the orchestral depth with motion"),
  ("Record parameter tweaks as automation while performing, and keep the version that best fits the character, place or story.","commit","A reusable signature sound for the cue")],
 ["A/B against dry: pitch/formant, saturation, filter and space each audibly change the sound.","An analyser shows a resonant bump at the cue's root (or chosen frequency) and reduced top end.","In context with an orchestra the transformed sound sits behind, not in front of, the ensemble.","The patch has audible movement (filter LFO or pan) rather than being static."],
 ["hybrid-score","orchestral","fx-chain","formant-shift","saturation","resonant-filter","reverb-pushback","sound-design"],
 note="Plugin names spoken in the video are examples only; the portable stage order is the skill. Credits and channel CTAs dropped.")

mk("ZVLM1XMT6Z0","granulate EVERYTHING",
 "key-mapped-grain-position-slicing-glide-between-slices-tempo-locked-grain-size-auto-recapture",
 "Granular slicer workflow: map keys to grain position instead of pitch (key→transpose 0, key→position up) with glide between slices, lock grain size to tempo in ms, per-parameter variation and L/R pitch spread for an icy cloud, an envelope that morphs grain shape and size per note, scan + per-ear LFOs for glitch, and a square-LFO auto-recapture of live drums every two bars",
 "Play any long recording (vocal, sound-design session, loop, live drums) as an ethereal slice instrument. Use three grain modes for different jobs: classic (overlapping, sustained), loop (non-overlapping, with reverse chance) and cloud (stacked grains, density). Set key→transpose to 0 and key→filter to 0, then turn key→position up so each key plays a different part of the file; enable mono + glide to glide between slices; smaller grains change position faster. Tune the pad version by checking the sample's pitch with a tuner. In cloud mode raise variation on grain size, small transposition (chorus) and volume, and add spread for different L/R pitch. Lock grain size to tempo by converting a note length to ms (a quarter note read as 408 ms). Remember transposition changes grain duration: 76.1 ms at 0 st becomes half at +12 st. Use the modulation envelope negatively on grain shape and size for plucky transients (decay ~1 s). Use scan (e.g. 0.25×) with LFOs (different per ear) on grain size and position for glitch, and auto-capture live drum input every two bars with a square, skewed LFO for generative texture. Delta vs our granular skills: key-to-position slicing with glide, tempo-ms grain lock and periodic self-recapture.",
 ["Granular sampler with grain size, position, scan, density, per-parameter variation, key modulation amounts and a capture-input mode (Granulator III is free in Live 12 Suite), or Web Audio AudioBufferSourceNode grain scheduler (implementation note)","Tuner, LFO, modulation envelope, optional live input/resampling routing"],
 [("source","audio","Vocal, sound-design session, loop or live drum input",True),("bpm","number","Tempo for grain-size lock",False),("key","object","Song key so slices sit in key (e.g. G major / E minor)",False)],
 [("slice_instrument","patch","Key-mapped granular slice instrument"),("textures","audio","Icy clouds, vocal chops, glitch loops, generative recaptured drum textures")],
 ["Spoken values only: quarter note = 408 ms example, grain 76.1 ms at 0 st halves at +12 st, scan 0.25×, envelope decay ~1 s, recapture every two bars.","Key→position mapping is not precisely quantifiable; audition amounts by ear.","MPE/note pitch bend still transposes even with key→transpose at 0.","Sponsored video; plugin pack promotion dropped."],
 [("Load a recording into the granular sampler and learn the three modes: classic (overlapping, sustained), loop (non-overlapping; set reverse chance) and cloud (stacked grains, density control).","modes","Hear sustained vs chopped vs dense cloud behaviour"),
  ("For a pad: check the sample's pitch with a tuner and transpose so C3 plays C. In cloud mode turn up variation, with orange per-parameter amounts on grain size, a little transposition (chorus) and volume; reduce position variation if too scattered. Add spread for different L/R pitch.","icy cloud","Wide, shimmering sustained pad"),
  ("For slicing: set key→transpose and key→filter to 0, set key→position up. Each key now plays a different region. Enable mono + glide to glide between slices; reduce grain size so position changes faster. Use it for vocal-chop patterns and transpose to the song key.","key→position","Keyboard becomes an ethereal slice player"),
  ("Tempo-lock grain size: measure a note length in ms (e.g. select a quarter note and read its duration, 408 ms in the example) and type it in. Remember +12 st transposition halves grain duration (76.1 → ~38 ms).","tempo","Grain repetition aligns with the beat"),
  ("Transient shaping: route the modulation envelope negatively to grain shape (pointy → regular) and to grain size (small → normal), decay ~1 s, in classic mode.","envelope","Plucky, articulated granular notes"),
  ("Glitch motion: set scan to ~0.25× and modulate grain size and position with an LFO that differs per ear. Record knob automation while playing.","scan + lfo","Glitchy, stereo-moving textures"),
  ("Generative recapture: feed live drum clips (random follow actions, legato on) into the granulator's capture input; drive capture with a square, skewed LFO every two bars; hold one note and scan slowly; map an LFO to grain size/shape. Resample the result to layer under the beat.","auto-capture","Ever-changing texture derived from the current groove")],
 ["With key→transpose 0, different keys play the same pitch but different sample regions.","Grain size set to the computed ms value repeats in time with the metronome.","With per-ear LFOs active, the left and right channels differ audibly.","Auto-capture refreshes the buffer every two bars (audible content change)."],
 ["granular","slicing","grain-position","tempo-sync","vocal-chops","generative","resampling","stereo"],
 note="Sponsored video; plugin pack promotion dropped.")
